<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Everything else is generated from INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings. This
	  file exists only for keys that mechanism cannot express: UIBackgroundModes
	  is not in its supported set, and setting INFOPLIST_KEY_UIBackgroundModes
	  silently does nothing.

	  remote-notification is required by CloudKit — without it the store never
	  receives the silent pushes that signal a change on another device, and
	  sync quietly degrades to pull-on-launch.

	  processing is what lets the week be written before she is awake to want
	  it — see PlanScheduler. Without the mode, submitting the task throws and
	  the plan simply waits for her to open the app, which is what it did
	  before.

	  Do not add "audio" here until the audio cue system actually exists. The
	  mode only keeps the process alive while a session is genuinely playing,
	  and declaring it without one is a review risk for no benefit.
	-->
	<!--
	  The design is single-register by construction: an oat ground, ink type
	  and a dark field that rises out of it. There is no dark variant and
	  there should not be one — inverting it would invert the field too, and
	  the register change *is* the design.

	  Declared rather than left to chance, because the system chrome does not
	  follow the palette. Unset, the tab bar in Dark Mode drew `Palette.ink`
	  on near-black — the selected tab was invisible at about 1.05:1, so she
	  could not tell which of the four screens she was on.
	-->
	<key>UIUserInterfaceStyle</key>
	<string>Light</string>

	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
		<string>processing</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Every identifier the app may register must be listed here or the
	  registration call traps at launch rather than failing — this is one of
	  the few Info.plist keys whose absence is a crash and not a degradation.
	  It must match PlanScheduler.taskIdentifier exactly.
	-->
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.zee.wrktime.plan</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  The launch screen is oat, not the generated white.

	  Opening the SwiftData store — now CloudKit-backed — happens before the
	  first frame, so the launch screen is genuinely on screen for a moment on a
	  cold start. Generated white against an oat app reads as a flash of the
	  wrong app. Painting it the app's own ground makes the gap invisible, which
	  is the right answer rather than a splash screen: a splash adds perceived
	  delay instead of hiding it, and Apple discourages them.
	-->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string>LaunchBackground</string>
	</dict>

	<!--
	  The backup file's own type. Declared so a restore can only be handed an
	  Almanac archive rather than any JSON on the device, and so the file has a
	  recognisable identity in Files and iCloud Drive. `UTType(exportedAs:)`
	  does not resolve without this declaration.
	-->
	<key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.zee.almanac.archive</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>Almanac Backup</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.json</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>almanac</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
